so I wanted to ask you about the origins  of the film it sounds like it was just  in a sense kind of your instinct to film  many documentaries the filmmakers don't  really have a way of knowing you know  the direction it may go  in this case you didn't even necessarily  have a documentary in mind initially  yeah you're right I I had no intention  of making a documentary when I first  started filming this  um you know when I moved back home from  New York I had all this free time on my  hands and uh prior to the pandemic I  always knew I wanted to share my  family's Story I mean you know if you  think about what my parents a  Mexican-American woman and this  Cambodian Refugee what they were able to  achieve and you know of all places bad  ax Michigan it's the American dream so I  always thought I would like write a  script and you know that would be my way  of sharing that story so I sat down in  those early days of the pandemic doing  two things and one of them was just  getting this whole oral history of how  you know the restaurant came to be what  it is today thinking this was just  research to write a script  um one day so you know just hearing how  like the restaurant came to be you know  not only a success but also like a  pinnacle of what badax Michigan is but  the other part is that instinct right it  was  um and I don't know I don't know if I  necessarily know what the Instinct was  other than knowing it was an important  time in in our lives and knowing I  wanted to just capture that on camera  for I think the reason being just having  these memories and Home Videos to hold  on to  um but I what I when I realized I had a  documentary it was that I wanted to make  a documentary I think was like shortly  after the black lives matter movement  happened  um when I began to see like these  parallel between these two things of  this history of this American dream I  just had on camera in these early days  but also like that our family was still  fighting so hard to keep that alive uh  in 2020 in the face of you know a  pandemic and racial Reckoning and  everything that was you know a  politically divided nation I mean it was  like oh this is the way to tell the  story of the American dream was a  documentary and that's just the way it  just naturally unfolded yeah the way  everything collided I think is just  opens a window onto the entire country  not simply yeah a small town in Michigan  I'm curious what it was like for you  growing up in bad acts because you know  as I think we see in the film and for  anyone who follows the news or social  media clearly the country is far more  polarized maybe than it ever has been so  things are getting worse in that respect  what was it like as a as a kid in that  town I mean you know life growing up in  bad acts  um  when I look back on it I am very  grateful for the childhood I had growing  up in the upbringing I had because you  know badax is such a it's it's like my  brother-in-law says it's such a  tight-knit community where everyone  knows everyone and  um and people do take care of each other  there  um having said that you know just like  any small town in anywhere in the U.S it  has its problems you know  um especially when it comes to racism  um so yeah you know I I experience  racism  um more often than anyone should growing  up uh you know being called Jackie Chan  and Bruce Lee and you know even being  called you know the n-word because my  skin is is a darker color like those  were all things I experienced growing up  um and I always felt embarrassed or or  ashamed of that which it took moving  away from Bad acts and gaining that  perspective of you know I guess like  going to Ann Arbor Michigan and living  in LA and living in New York knowing  that like why am I embarrassed of that  you know  um I'm actually the one that's not the  problem here it's it's the the way of  thinking that some of these individuals  have in this community that that needs  to open up and needs to change  um but you know that's that's what it  came with growing up in bad acts and  those experiences you know I say it's a  love letter to bad X because it really  is  um  that that love is unconditional because  it's what made me who I am today and  it's what made our family who we are  today you know you take the good with  the bad with that  and you mentioned the black lives matter  protest which is such an extraordinary  scene and very upsetting I think for the  audience to watch uh let alone for you  to be there in the midst of it but  whether it was that scene or others what  was um the hardest parts of of making  the film was that those kind of moments  or  senior father be so vulnerable at times  on camera which  a little I don't know scary is the word  for Hassan to see that but yeah it was  God it was It was kind of all of it I  mean  um you know speaking of my dad first I  mean he always shared his stories about  surviving the genus you know the Killing  Fields growing up  um but what he didn't ever really share  was how those stories affected who he is  as a dad as the person who he is today  it really took those hour-long  conversations of having the camera  pointed at him for him to really open up  about that and I don't know if that was  more hard for me as more I was just  thankful actually really grateful he was  willing to share those emotions he never  did before with me because you know  during the pandemic he had all this free  time on his hands just like we all did  right and it gave him a lot of time to  think and  yes he's a Storyteller but like I said  like it's it's one thing to tell these  stories it's another thing to talk about  like how they actually you know have  affected who you are in the trauma that  comes with that  um so I was grateful he was willing to  open up about that stuff  um but I think you know like if we're  talking about like the more difficult  sides of making this film it really came  along in the editing process  um  it's easy for me to pick up a camera and  film these moments you know it it truly  is because that's what I've just kind of  done my whole life it's another thing to  take those moments and start editing  them together to tell a story and  especially when that story is is about  your family because you know as you've  seen my family is very opinionated  um no  um so everybody had their own ideas  about what the film should be and you  had some co-directors I had a lot of  co-directors  um but I'll say that you know like in  those early editing days  um I I think I I kind of lost track of  eyes in this film in the first place and  wanted to be this filmmaker that stood  up on my soapbox and  he you know pointed out everything that  was wrong with our country you're just  yelling into the echo Chambers and you  know my family was the one that's like  you're not gonna change people's you  know you're not gonna open up people's  hearts by doing that like why are you  even doing this in the first place and  it really took those tough conversations  with them to come to the answer to that  which was  um  were actually quite simple which was the  love I had for my family and wanting to  share their story of resilience and  coming back to the answer of love I  think is what uh the film needed in  order for it to you know take the final  um form that it currently has because  you know with this film I I  when I came to that conclusion I  realized like I can't preach to people I  I don't need to preach to people I just  need to show you know and tell this  personal story that I wanted to start  doing from the first place and then  these like messages and these themes  naturally unveil themselves in a way  where  um through an empathetic lens versus you  know like trying to tell you you're  wrong if you disagree because the film  you know at the end of the day it's not  trying to do that it's just trying to  show an experience that might be  different from others you know growing  up in towns like bad ax Michigan things  that fascinated me about the film you  know you begin it with that letter  that was sent and sort of a happy font  style almost so I was like is that comic  sensor Sans serif and only it was but it  it sort of belied the the the tone of it  yeah but the person saying that you know  we stand for traditional American values  yes but what are those values yeah  that's that's something that really got  to me because like I think in the  process of making this film  um one of the things I really want  audiences to walk away with is looking  at my family as being more American than  any other family because you know when  when people think of the Civ family you  know my family they might not think of  us as your traditional American family  but the reality is that America is a  country that's built on immigrants and  refugees and what my family experience  during the pandemic make was what so  many other families from from you know  not even backgrounds like ours but from  all backgrounds experience right and  like so therefore like that's the  American experience and  you know with that you know  um I I hope like you know when people  view our family it opens up their idea  of what the American family is you know  so when people you know when we got that  letter and it's like traditional  American values it's like what is that  those are your traditional American  values it's not everyone's you know  traditional American values it's like  when Jacqueline says like bad X is still  our hometown  it's true it's just as much as my home  as it is any of my neighbors home you  know  um and that was that really played the  influence in naming the film bad X too  um because at first we're like oh like  are people gonna be turned off that Civ  family you know we're the ones making a  film called badax because we're far  different than anyone else in our  community but then I thought about like  no I have every right to make a film  called bad X and anybody else in this  town  yeah when I first saw the title I  thought wow that's a great name for a  horror film  we get that a lot but you know it  creates Intrigue which I guess is not a  bad thing you know I'm sure there's a  story about how the town got named maybe  for another time but um how do you think  the film has sort of the process of  making it and then bring it out into the  world and go into so many film festivals  which by the way it's won many awards  including your world premiere at South  by Southwest where you won an award in  the audience award  um but how is it sort of changed your  family or do you think it has I think  one of the things documentaries can do  of that nature is people get to say  things that they just wouldn't otherwise  yeah I I honestly say that you know we  were a close family  um even before making this and now that  the film has been out there and we're on  this journey of promoting the film  together as a family it's really only  brought us that much closer together  um you know we're I was talking about  earlier we're in the middle of a 45-day  road trip right now just across the  country promoting the film and we're so  grateful you know my parents never got  to travel at all  um uh prior to this year they our idea  of vacation was driving three hours of  Toronto and spending two days there and  now they've seen every corner of the  country in the past month and we've  gotten to just you know spend time  together and celebrate and share a story  and have conversations with other people  it's something that you know I I'm so  grateful for and  um to be able to do that together as a  family is just it's just made us that  much closer and much so much stronger  together  um I I don't take any of it for granted  I don't think they do either  well it's a tremendous film I personally  speak for myself think it's one without  a doubt one of the finest documentaries  of the year and one that I think is  really gonna Gonna Last and let's touch  so many people I'm sure others in the  audience tonight as well so let's thank  David Zeb for this thank you so much  thank you for sticking around  [Applause]

